id: hype
name: Hype
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Play to the crowd. You are Hyped until the end of your next turn: 1(T) plus a
  quarter of your Personality Modifier on every Combat Roll.
text: >
  Hype Maneuver [1/Round]: Hype the crowds, your allies, or yourself with a powerful
  performance.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard Maneuver
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –Minions: Free
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: N/A
  –Effect: You become Hyped! While Hyped, increase your Combat Rolls by 1(T)+1/4
  (rounded up) of your Personality Modifier until the end of your next turn.

---

# Two lines and no code, which is the first Maneuver in a while that needed nothing
# but its own file. Everything it does is about the character using it, which is what
# this language is for - the Analysis Maneuver needed a flag because both halves of it
# were about a pair.
#
# `gain` puts the mark on and `expires` starts its clock. "Until the end of your next
# turn", used on your own turn, is two turn edges away: the end of this one and the end
# of the next. The clock counts them, which it did not until the version before this -
# a duration of more than one edge was stepped down and the step thrown away.
#
# What being Hyped is worth is in conditions/hyped.dbu rather than here. It is a rule
# about whoever is Hyped, and a passive on the mark is exactly that - so anything else
# that ever makes somebody Hyped gets the same bonus without being told about it.
[on used]
gain(hyped);
expires(hyped, next-turn);

# --- What is not built, and why -----------------------------------------------------
#
# "Minions: Free." There are no Minions in this system, which the Arrogant Declaration
# file says about its own Minion limit and for the same reason: there is no notion here
# of whose Minions somebody is. The line is carried as printed and what it buys is the
# table's.
#
# "Hype the crowds, your allies, or yourself" is the tagline rather than the Effect,
# and the Effect names one beneficiary: you. Nothing here reaches an Ally, because the
# rule does not.
